# The control plane issues identity, holds the machine registry, and relays every
# request to every machine in the fleet.
#
# It is never consulted to verify anything. Daemons contact it exactly once, at
# enrollment, and never again; every token after that is checked locally against
# a public key they already hold. This service being down cannot stop a token
# verifying, a session running, or a daemon starting.
#
# It *is* on the data path, and that half changed: users do not reach machines
# directly. Every request goes down the tunnel its daemon dialled out to here, so
# an outage of this service costs all reachability — while still costing nothing
# that is already running, which is the distinction the paragraph above draws.
#
# One thing is required to start: REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_URL, below. The first run
# mints an admin user, prints its password and its API key once, and generates the
# Ed25519 signing key.

# Where durable state lives: users, API-key hashes, machines, grants, enrollment
# codes, and the private key that signs every token in the fleet. That last item
# is why this file is created 0600 inside a 0700 directory — losing it is not
# "somebody reads your transcripts", it is "somebody is every user everywhere".
# Default: ~/.reemoat/control-plane.db
# REEMOAT_CP_DB=

# Where to bind. 127.0.0.1 by default, because this service holds the signing key
# and exposing it should be a deliberate act. The *relay* has its own listener and
# its own opposite default, which is the whole reason they are two ports.
#
# Note that this also serves the web UI, so a phone reaching the UI at all means
# binding wider than loopback — a Tailnet address rather than 0.0.0.0 if you have
# the choice, because of what is in the database.
#
# **Under Docker this key is inert, and the one below replaces it.** Said here
# rather than left to be discovered, because a line that looks load-bearing and
# is not is worse than no line. `deploy/docker/compose.yml` pins both bind
# addresses to 0.0.0.0 in its `environment:` block — which beats `env_file:`,
# measured — because inside a network namespace a loopback bind means "reachable
# by nobody" and the failure has no symptom from inside. Only `pnpm cp` still
# reads these.
# REEMOAT_CP_HOST=127.0.0.1
# REEMOAT_CP_PORT=7888

# Who can reach the service, under Docker — the host side of the published port.
#
# The decision `REEMOAT_CP_HOST` used to make, one layer out, with the same
# defaults and the same reasoning: loopback for the API because it carries
# /v1/admin/* and the key that mints every token, every interface for the relay
# because a relay daemons cannot dial is not a relay. Separate keys rather than
# one, so that publishing the relay cannot publish the API with it.
#
# The port is deliberately the same number on both sides of the mapping, so
# REEMOAT_CP_PORT above stays the single answer to "what port is this on" for
# the health probe, for cpctl.env and for REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_URL.
#
# Write an *interface* address rather than 0.0.0.0 when widening the first one.
# On Linux a published port is a DNAT rule in nat/PREROUTING, evaluated before
# the filter/INPUT chain where ufw and firewalld write theirs — so `ufw deny
# 7888` does not deny it, while naming one address here means nothing else can
# reach it and no firewall has to cooperate. On macOS this does not apply.
# REEMOAT_CP_PUBLISH=127.0.0.1
# REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_PUBLISH=0.0.0.0

# How many reverse proxies of your own stand in front of the API.
#
# This decides how much of `x-forwarded-for` is believed, and therefore what
# every rate limiter here keys on: the login throttle, the per-address backstop,
# the registration and confirmation counters, and the new one on /v1/enroll.
#
# 0, the default, ignores the header outright and uses the socket. That is the
# safe answer and the wrong one for the shape recommended above — publishing on
# 127.0.0.1 with TLS in front — where it puts every caller in one bucket, so one
# person's failed sign-ins refuse everybody else's. If you did that, set 1. Two
# would be your own proxy behind a CDN.
#
# Entries are counted from the **right**, because that is the end a proxy
# appends to: with one hop the last entry is what your proxy observed, while the
# leftmost is whatever the client sent. Reading the left is what this replaced,
# and it let anybody pick their own bucket — or spell somebody else's and lock
# that address out of signing in for fifteen minutes. A header with fewer
# entries than hops did not come through the chain you described, so the socket
# answers instead.
#
# The API warns once, at runtime, if a request arrives with the header while
# this is 0.
# REEMOAT_CP_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=0

# The `iss` claim, and what enrolled daemons check every token against.
# Changing it after daemons have enrolled means re-enrolling all of them.
# REEMOAT_CP_ISSUER=reemoat-cp

# How long an issued token lives.
#
# **No longer the revocation window for a grant.** It was, and it could not be
# separated: daemons never ask this service anything, so a revocation reached only
# as far as the next mint. The relay is now the only path in and it checks live
# user, machine and grant rows before a byte enters the tunnel, so revoking takes
# effect on the *next request*.
#
# What this still bounds is a WebSocket already open, which the daemon closes on
# its own ping tick at exp + leeway. Five minutes is the default; lower it to
# shorten that, raise it to survive a longer outage of this service.
#
# Refused below 120s. The daemon accepts a token over [nbf-60s, exp+60s], so
# beneath twice that leeway the stated lifetime stops meaning anything — a 60s
# token would really last 180s.
# REEMOAT_CP_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=300

# --- the relay ---
#
# **Required.** This service refuses to start without REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_URL,
# because every daemon dials it and every request to a machine goes through it —
# a control plane without one is a fleet nobody can reach.
#
# It is the one piece of configuration that cannot be guessed: this process has
# no way to know what hostname daemons and browsers reach it by, and that URL is
# handed out at enrollment and with every token. Deriving it from the bind
# address would produce http://0.0.0.0:7889, which works nowhere.
#
# What it buys: a daemon behind NAT, with no inbound ports and no public address,
# dials out over WSS and holds one connection. Everyone reaches it through here.
# What makes it different from ngrok or a Cloudflare tunnel is the authorization:
# every request is checked against the caller's signed token AND their grant on
# that machine, against live rows, before a single byte enters the tunnel — which
# is why revoking a grant takes effect on the next request.
#
# A separate listener from the API above, on purpose, and the split matters more
# now that this one has to face the internet: /v1/admin/* does not, and one port
# would publish both.
REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_URL=https://relay.example
# REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_HOST=0.0.0.0
# REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_PORT=7889

# Which process holds the tunnels. `embedded` (the default) or `external`.
#
# **This is not a switch for turning the relay off** — there is no such thing, and
# REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_URL above is still required either way. It says only *where*
# the listener is.
#
# `embedded` is one process, exactly as this service has always run, and is what
# `pnpm cp` starts.
#
# `external` says a separate relay process owns the listener, and this one reads
# which machines are connected out of the `relay_tunnels` table instead of out of
# its own memory. That is what `deploy/docker/compose.yml` runs, and the reason is
# restart cost: recreating this service is ordinary — a web bundle moved, a route
# changed — and as one container it took every tunnel in the fleet with it, which
# is tens of seconds of reconnecting for every open session and every in-flight
# request lost. The relay's own inputs move rarely, so splitting them is what
# makes a backend deploy free.
#
# The relay process reads REEMOAT_CP_DB, REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_HOST/PORT,
# REEMOAT_CP_ISSUER and REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_ID out of this same file. The issuer
# especially: the two must agree or every token fails `iss` at the relay.
# REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_MODE=embedded

# The relay's name in `relay_tunnels` — a deployment slot, not a process.
#
# A relay that is killed hard cannot delete its own rows, so its replacement
# clears them by this name at boot. That only works while the name survives a
# restart, which is why it is a fixed default rather than anything derived from a
# pid or a container id.
#
# **Give each relay its own** once there is more than one. Two relays sharing an
# id would delete each other's rows on every five-second flush, so a relay that
# finds its id already held by a live process **refuses to start** rather than
# joining the fight. The claim is released on SIGTERM, so an ordinary restart
# reclaims the name with no wait; only a hard kill costs a 20s window.
#
# It is also the key REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_URLS below is written against, so it is
# what decides where a browser is sent. See deploy/RELAYS.md.
# REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_ID=relay

# Where a *browser* reaches each relay, once there is more than one.
#
# <relay-id>=<url>, comma separated. Unset is the shape every deployment has
# until somebody runs a second relay: one relay, and REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_URL above
# is where everybody goes.
#
# The two are different questions, which is why this is not a plural of that
# one. REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_URL is the name *daemons* dial; it is written into each
# daemon's identity at enrollment and never asked about again, so it must stay
# one value pointing at whatever fronts the relays. This is the name a *browser*
# dials, and it has to be the specific relay holding that machine's tunnel,
# because the map of live tunnels is in-memory per process — land on the wrong
# one and you get 503 no_tunnel.
#
# The ids are each relay's own REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_ID, which is what it stamps into
# relay_tunnels.relay_id. A slot with no entry here falls back to the shared
# name rather than to nothing.
#
# **http:// or https://, never ws:// or wss://.** The client probes a route with
# fetch(), which rejects a wss: URL outright, and derives the socket scheme
# itself — https becomes wss and anything else becomes plain ws. So a wss://
# value here does not fail loudly, it downgrades the stream. Refused at startup.
#
# Only read when REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_MODE is external: an embedded API holds the
# tunnels itself and can only route to itself, so it warns and ignores this.
#
# See deploy/RELAYS.md before running a second relay — the order of operations
# matters, and one of the steps cannot be redone later.
# REEMOAT_CP_RELAY_URLS=relay-1=https://r1.example,relay-2=https://r2.example

# Name of the admin user minted on first start, when there are no users at all.
#
# It is also a *login* now, so pick something less guessable than the default on a
# service that faces the internet: it is the account with the most privilege and
# the one an attacker will try first. Letters, digits and . _ - only.
#
# Its API key and its password are both printed once, to stdout, and only their
# hashes are kept. The key survives because it is what `cpctl` uses, what
# `deploy/install.sh` captures into ~/.reemoat/cpctl.env, and the only credential
# that still works if this service is ever rolled back past the release that added
# passwords.
# REEMOAT_CP_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN=admin

# The bootstrap admin's password. Read **only** when there are no users at all.
#
# Leave it unset and one is generated and printed once, which is the recommended
# path — `deploy/install.sh` catches it for you. Set it and it is used silently and
# never echoed.
#
# **Prefer leaving it unset**, and the reason is about this file rather than about
# passwords: compose reads it on every command, `deploy/compose.sh config` prints
# it, and it sits in the container's environment for the life of the process — all
# for a value that is used once, on a database that already exists by the second
# start. If you do set one, delete the line after you have signed in.
#
# Refused at startup if it fails the policy (at least 12 characters, and not the
# same as the admin's name) rather than quietly stored as something else. Also
# refused by `set_env` if it contains an apostrophe: this file is parsed by *two*
# grammars — `sh`, via run-cp.sh, and docker compose's dotenv — and only the first
# understands the POSIX escape, so one apostrophe makes the whole stack refuse to
# start or even be inspected.
# REEMOAT_CP_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# --- the web UI ---
#
# Served from this origin, from packages/web/dist, by default — build it with
# `pnpm --filter @reemoat/web build`. A missing directory is not an error: the
# API has no business depending on a bundler having run.
#
# The assets are public, deliberately. There is no secret in the bundle; the
# credential is obtained by signing in, lives in localStorage, and is sent only
# back here — never to a daemon and never to the relay. It is a bearer token
# rather than a cookie, so nothing is ever carried by the browser on its own
# initiative, which is what keeps `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` safe.
#
# Set to 0 to serve no UI at all, or to a path to serve a different build.
#
# ⚠ If you serve the UI from somewhere else — a CDN, nginx, another origin —
# **you are taking over the security headers with it.** They are set here, on the
# HTML response, by `app.ts`, and a document this process does not send is a
# document this process cannot header. At minimum your server has to send:
#
#   Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self';
#     style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' blob:; font-src 'self';
#     connect-src 'self' <your control plane origin> <relay https origin> <relay wss origin>;
#     object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
#   X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
#   Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
#   X-Frame-Options: DENY
#   Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
#
# `connect-src` is the one that cannot be copied verbatim: this page is
# deliberately cross-origin to the fleet, and `https` and `wss` are different
# sources to CSP, so listing one is listing neither. `frame-ancestors 'none'` is
# not decoration either — the UI approves shell commands with a tap, so a page
# that can be framed is a page where somebody else chooses what you approve.
# REEMOAT_CP_WEB=

# --- for cpctl, the terminal client ---
# REEMOAT_CP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:7888
# REEMOAT_CP_KEY=rk_…

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ #
# Registration and outgoing mail
#
# Every one of these is a **seed**, not the value. A row in `instance_settings`
# — written from Settings → Server settings — wins over anything here, and the
# admin screen shows which of the two won for every field so a shadowed
# environment variable is visible rather than baffling. Clearing the override in
# the UI hands the value back to this file.
#
# Nothing seeds the table at startup, deliberately: `schema.sql` is re-applied on
# every open, so a seed would overwrite an admin's change at every restart and
# the symptom would read as "registration turns itself back on after a deploy".
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ #

# Whether people may create their own accounts. Default closed.
#
# With mail configured a sign-up must confirm an address before the account
# exists at all; without it, a name and a password create one immediately and
# nothing verifies who they are.
#
# ⚠ Opening this means strangers can reach the port that also carries
#   /v1/admin/* and, behind it, the key that mints every token in the fleet.
#   That port is already published on any deployment somebody uses from a phone
#   — the web UI is served from it — but on Linux a published port is a DNAT
#   rule evaluated *before* the chain ufw and firewalld write to, so `ufw deny`
#   will not take it back. Publish on one address, behind a TLS proxy.
# REEMOAT_CP_REGISTRATION_ENABLED=false

# Comma-separated domains registration will accept. Empty means any address.
# A refused address reads exactly like a malformed one — the list is never
# published, so it cannot be used to learn whose instance this is.
# REEMOAT_CP_REGISTRATION_EMAIL_DOMAINS=

# How many machines one person may own.
#
# **The commercial bound, and not the anti-abuse one.** MAX_MACHINES_PER_USER in
# machines.ts is 50 and is a hard ceiling this may not exceed — a machine is a
# database row plus an enrollment code plus a tunnel credential, against a
# `synchronous = FULL` file in the process that carries every tunnel. A value
# above 50 is refused. An admin raises or lowers it for **one person** under
# Settings → Users, which writes a row that beats this file for that account
# alone.
#
# ⚠ Lowering it deletes nothing. The machines somebody acquired most recently
#   stop working — oldest kept, newest first to go — and they start working
#   again the moment the number goes back up. Nothing is stored as "suspended",
#   so there is no state to repair and no order to do things in.
#
# ⚠ Unset means 50, which is the behaviour before this setting existed, and it
#   is deliberately not 0. This file is read by instances that are already
#   running with machines enrolled: a default of 0 would take every machine in
#   the fleet offline at the next deploy, with the relay refusing every tunnel
#   and no operator having done anything. Choosing 0 is how you close an
#   instance; arriving at 0 by upgrading would be a fault.
#
# ⚠ The relay reads this too, out of its own environment. compose.yml gives the
#   API and the relay one env_file so they cannot disagree; a relay on another
#   host has its own `environment:` block (deploy/RELAYS.md) and can. Setting
#   the value in Settings → Server settings instead writes a row, which both
#   processes read out of the one database.
# REEMOAT_CP_MACHINES_PER_USER=50

# The mailbox this service sends from, and the address links point at.
#
# `MAIL_FROM` is both the `From:` header and the envelope sender. Most
# submission servers insist it is the mailbox you authenticated as, or one of
# its aliases, and answer `550 sender not allowed` otherwise — so for a rented
# mailbox these two are usually the same string. A relay that authorises a whole
# verified domain is the case where they differ, which is why a mismatch is a
# warning on the settings screen rather than a refusal.
#
# `MAIL_PUBLIC_URL` is where a confirmation or reset link points: the address a
# person opens in a browser, not the API's bind address. Without it there is
# nowhere for a link to go and mail counts as unconfigured.
# REEMOAT_CP_MAIL_FROM=register@example.com
# REEMOAT_CP_MAIL_FROM_NAME=reemoat
# REEMOAT_CP_MAIL_REPLY_TO=
# REEMOAT_CP_MAIL_PUBLIC_URL=https://cp.example.com

# The submission server.
#
# `SECURITY` is starttls (usually 587), implicit_tls (usually 465), or plaintext
# — which exists for a loopback dev MTA and refuses to send a password.
#
# Port 587 rather than 25 by default because **outbound 25 is blocked by AWS,
# GCP, DigitalOcean, Hetzner and Azure**, and a blocked port does not refuse: it
# hangs until the connect timeout, which is the most expensive failure this
# service has.
#
# `USERNAME` is almost always the *full* mailbox address rather than the part
# before the @. `AUTH` is plain, login, or none; `none` is how a server that
# wants no credential says so — an empty username does not say it, and an empty
# one with AUTH on would send `AUTH PLAIN \0\0password`, which is a malformed
# sign-in rather than an absent one.
# REEMOAT_CP_SMTP_HOST=mail.example.com
# REEMOAT_CP_SMTP_PORT=587
# REEMOAT_CP_SMTP_SECURITY=starttls
# REEMOAT_CP_SMTP_USERNAME=register@example.com
# REEMOAT_CP_SMTP_AUTH=plain
# REEMOAT_CP_SMTP_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=true

# ⚠ The password is better set in the UI than here, and not for taste.
#
# This file is read by docker compose on *every* command and printed in full by
# `deploy/compose.sh config`, exactly as REEMOAT_CP_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD's
# own paragraph above says. And `set_env` **refuses an apostrophe** in this file
# — compose's dotenv grammar cannot take the POSIX escape and rejects the whole
# file, taking build, up, ps, logs and config down together — which is
# unfollowable advice for a password you did not choose.
#
# Settings → Server settings writes it to the database instead, where it is
# never returned by any route: the read path reports a boolean and never the
# value, never its length, and never a masked prefix.
# REEMOAT_CP_SMTP_PASSWORD=
